# One exit code per kind of answer, across the tools.
#
# A caller that treats every non-zero exit alike cannot tell a protocol
# it should fix from an invocation it should fix. The codes are the
# machine-readable half of that distinction and the prose is not, so
# they are pinned here rather than left to whichever helper a site
# happened to call (docs/getting-started.md §4a):
#
#   0  the question was answered affirmatively
#   1  the subject was examined and the answer is negative
#   2  the invocation never reached its subject
#
# Both non-zero codes write to stderr in the same spelling, so this
# checks the code and not the wording. `not --crash` is lit's way of
# asserting a specific non-zero status: plain `not` accepts any of
# them, which is exactly the conflation this file exists to refuse.

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t

# -- 2: the invocation never reached its subject. ---------------------
# An unreadable file, whatever the tool would have done with it.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-registry-lint %t/absent.json
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-family %t/absent.json --emit-vocabulary=%t/v.json --emit-spine=%t/s.mlir
# A flag set that names no job.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-translate %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-run %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir
# A contract the environment supplied does not carry.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-relation %S/../Encoding/schnorr.mlir --contracts=%zkc-registry-dir/relation-contracts.json --contract=no.such.contract --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json

# A file the tool cannot take at all. These four were the review's
# finding: each reported its mistake through the exit-2 channel, threw
# the code away, and returned 1 from the caller — so the message named
# an invocation error and the status said the subject had been examined.
# RUN: echo 'not a module' > %t/notmlir.mlir
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-seal %t/notmlir.mlir --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json --output-dir=%t/out
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-translate --id %t/notmlir.mlir
# A profile the environment does not carry, and a malformed payload flag.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-run %S/../Oir/Inputs/schnorr-verifier.mlir --profile=no-such-profile --vectors=%t/absent.json
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 2 zkc-run %S/../Oir/Inputs/schnorr-verifier.mlir --prove malformed --statement y=1

# -- 1: the subject was examined and refused. -------------------------
# A registry that does not admit is this tool's whole question, so its
# parse refusal is an answer rather than a failure to reach one.
# RUN: echo '{"registry":"zkc.protocol_vocabulary","unknown_section":1}' > %t/bad.json
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 1 zkc-registry-lint %t/bad.json
# A description outside the family's closed point selection.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 1 zkc-family %S/../Family/Inputs/bad-k.json --emit-vocabulary=%t/v.json --emit-spine=%t/s.mlir
# A protocol that parses and that the seal battery refuses — the case
# that keeps the fixes above from having simply moved everything to 2.
# RUN: %python -c "import re,sys,pathlib; s=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_text(); pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).write_text(re.sub(r'(anchors .\{statement = \"sha256:)([0-9a-f]{4})', r'\g<1>dead', s, count=1))" %S/../Encoding/routed-schnorr.mlir %t/battery-refuses.mlir
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 1 zkc-seal %t/battery-refuses.mlir --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json --output-dir=%t/out2

# -- 0: answered affirmatively. ---------------------------------------
# The control: without it the checks above are satisfied by a tool that
# refuses everything.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/exit-code.py 0 zkc-registry-lint %zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json
